; Public Suffix List fixtures live outside .test on purpose.
;
; .test gets the PSL's implicit "*" rule, so org-domain(sub.foo.test) == foo.test
; for free — which is the behaviour DMARC needs and a happy accident. What .test
; cannot model is a *multi-label* public suffix, and those are where the bugs are:
; DMARC is only valid at PSL+1, so `_dmarc.example.co.uk` counts and
; `_dmarc.co.uk` must never be consulted.
;
; These zones are reachable only when a test targets dns-auth directly. They are
; deliberately not delegated from the fake root, because the fake root has no
; uk. or com. and inventing one would model the wrong thing.
;
; Phase 1 consequence: the PSL has to be vendored as generated TypeScript to keep
; @propgate/dns at zero runtime dependencies, with a refresh script.
$ORIGIN example.co.uk.
$TTL 300

@       IN SOA  ns1.test. hostmaster.propgate.invalid. (
                1 7200 3600 1209600 300 )
@       IN NS   ns1.test.
@       IN A    198.51.100.90

_dmarc          IN TXT  "v=DMARC1; p=reject; sp=quarantine; rua=mailto:d@example.co.uk"
@               IN TXT  "v=spf1 include:spf.propgate-fixture.test -all"

; Subdomain with its own _dmarc, which DMARC lookup must ignore in favour of the
; organizational domain's policy plus sp=.
sub             IN A    198.51.100.91
_dmarc.sub      IN TXT  "v=DMARC1; p=none"
